<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#millet">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Millet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Millet is a group of hardy, drought-tolerant cereal grains grown widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It was a staple crop of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Han and Ming dynasties and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> states of Africa, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> states. Its resilience made it essential to agricultural economies in dry regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kurt Stüber [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panicum_miliaceum0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Millets are a varied group of small-seeded grasses grown as a staple cereal and used as fodder for livestock and as food for humans. Most belong to the tribe Paniceae, but the species are not all closely related. Common kinds include pearl millet, the most widely grown, proso, foxtail and finger millet, and fonio. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">Sorghum</a> is sometimes called great millet. Millets tolerate drought and extreme weather and give a crop in a short season under hot, dry conditions, and their nutrition is comparable to wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Millets may have been eaten for about 7,000 years. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proso and foxtail millet were important from the Early Neolithic, and the earliest evidence is at Cishan, where proso millet remains in storage pits date to roughly 10,300 to 8,700 years ago. Millet was farmed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from about 3500 BCE. Little millet and kodo millet were domesticated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> by about 3000 and 3700 BCE.</p><p>Finger millet is native to the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and pearl millet was domesticated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, with early finds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhar-tichitt">Dhar Tichitt</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and in the Tilemsi valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. Proso millet reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 1500 BCE and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1200 BCE. Hesiod and Theophrastus mention it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Millet is a staple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, making up much of the cereal eaten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>, and it can be ground and mixed with milk to make fura and brukina. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> pearl millet, there called mahangu, is the dominant food staple. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> it was a common food of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a>, as a porridge called kulish.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> it is usually eaten as a flour, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> it is fermented into drinks such as tongba and raksi. It is also a forage crop, on which lambs gain weight faster than on forage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Millet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Soba the main cereal was sorghum, also brewed into beer, with barley and <b>millet</b>; figs, dates, acacia and doum palm fruits and grapes have been found, though al-Aswani noted that vineyards were rare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Baekje's core lay in the fertile plains of the Han, Geum and Yeongsan river basins, where rice grown in irrigated paddies, together with <b>millet</b> and barley, formed the basis of the economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bahmani economy rested on agriculture in the black cotton soils and river valleys of the Deccan, producing <b>millets</b>, rice, cotton and oilseeds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Farmers grew <b>millet</b> and sorghum, rice on the floodplains and cotton, which women spun late into the night, as Park observed; Fulbe herders kept cattle, and Bozo and Somono fishermen and boatmen worked the river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Farmers cultivated gardens on the fertile margins and on raised mounds in the plain, growing sorghum and <b>millet</b> and later maize and cassava; large herds of cattle grazed the plain when the waters fell, and fish were caught in weirs and traps as the flood retreated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on the black cotton soils of the plateau, which produced cotton, <b>millet</b> and wheat, and on textile weaving and the trade of ports on the Konkan coast such as Dabhol.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
